@startuml flow
' The end-to-end flow a person follows: get ready -> find the device -> install -> test.
' Nodes stay short + robust; the exact copy-paste commands live in the README/requirements text.
title How to set up and test a SelfPrivacy box — 3 steps

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #EEF3FB
  BorderColor #5a6b7a
}

start
:<b>0. Get ready</b> (once)
cable the new device to this laptop, turn it on in "network boot" mode
(it shows its own MAC on screen), then start the address server:
sudo bash ~/netboot/start-netboot-server.sh;
:<b>1. Find the device</b>
run  <b>./dash find-target</b>
→ it prints the device's MAC + IP, e.g.  MAC 80:e8:2c:15:81:8b  IP 192.168.100.29;
:<b>2. Install onto it</b>
run  <b>./dash run install.lan-setup-0d ...</b>  with the MAC + IP from step 1,
your web address, and your wifi name + password (full command below the picture)
→ it asks "erase which disk?", then installs and prints <b>done</b>;
:<b>3. Test the running box</b>
run  <b>./dash run L3.connect.desktop ... --token ...</b>
→ <b>green = it works</b>   (red = something is off, the log says what);
note right
  <b>Where your secrets go</b>
  The wifi password, the app secret key (token) and the TLS
  certificate are written only into the installer's <b>state/</b>
  folder on THIS computer. It is never uploaded or pushed
  (git-ignored + excluded from the backup). To back it up too,
  lock the whole folder with ONE password:  <b>age -p</b>.
end note
stop
@enduml
